---
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro'
import Star from '../../components/Star.astro'
import '../../styles/tools.css'
import { categories, tools, used, roles, usedPending, checked, unreachable, moved } from '../../lib/tools'

const edges = tools.reduce((n, t) => n + t.mentions.length, 0)
const withGraph = categories.filter((c) => {
  const inSet = new Set(c.tools.map((t) => t.slug))
  const nodes = new Set<string>()
  for (const t of c.tools) for (const m of t.mentions) if (inSet.has(m)) { nodes.add(t.slug); nodes.add(m) }
  return nodes.size >= 2 && nodes.size <= 16
}).length
---
<Doc
  title="Tools"
  description={`${tools.length} tools, described here rather than quoted, and ${used.length} with evidence that they build or serve this site. Every homepage checked on ${checked}.`}>

  <div class="mast">
    <div>Off the Bit
<b>Tools</b>
Category: Catalogue
Count: {used.length} with evidence, {tools.length} catalogued</div>
    <div class="r"><b>M. Imran</b>
Index: <a href="/">draft</a>
Checked: {checked}</div>
  </div>

  <div class="title">
    <h1>Tools</h1>
    <div class="sub">Two lists, kept apart on purpose. One is short and carries evidence. The other is long and carries no claim at all.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="ind tsec">
    <div class="memo">The difference between the two halves is the only thing here worth being
      careful about. <b>Used here</b> is {used.length} tools, and every row names the file in this
      repository that proves it. <b>Catalogued</b> is {tools.length} tools described from their own
      homepages: what each one is, what it is sold for, and whether it still answers. None of that
      is a claim that anyone here has used them. Merging the two would read as fluency across six
      hundred products, which nobody has.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="ind tsec">
    <h2 id="find">Find</h2>
    <form class="tfind" method="get" action="/tools/search">
      <label class="vh" for="q">Search the catalogue</label>
      <input id="q" name="q" type="search" placeholder="postgres, terminal, code review" autocomplete="off" required>
      <button type="submit">Search</button>
    </form>
    <p class="dim" style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12px;margin:2px 0 0">
      Searches names and descriptions, answered server-side. The reading path here ships no
      JavaScript, and a search box that needs a bundle to work is a search box this site cannot have.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ind tsec">
    <h2 id="used">Used here</h2>
    <p class="prose">These build, check or serve this site. The evidence line is the point: each one
      names a file you can open in the repository and confirm, rather than asking you to take a
      rating on trust. They are the only starred entries on the site.</p>
    <ul class="tl">
      {used.map((u) => (
        <li>
          <div class="h">
            <Star title={u.evidence} />
            <a href={u.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{u.name}</a>
            <span class="trole">{u.role}</span>
          </div>
          {u.note && <div class="d">{u.note}</div>}
          <div class="tev"><b>Evidence:</b> {u.evidence}</div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="prose"><b>What the three words mean.</b>
      {Object.entries(roles).map(([k, v]) => (<><br /><b>{k}</b> {v}</>))}</p>
    <p class="prose dim">{usedPending}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ind tsec">
    <h2 id="categories">By category</h2>
    <p class="prose">Fourteen shelves. Each page lists everything on it, alphabetically, with a
      drawing where the entries actually reference each other and a note where they do not.</p>
    <div class="tcats">
      {categories.map((c) => (
        <a class="tcat" href={`/tools/in/${c.key}/`}>
          <span class="n">{c.tools.length}</span>
          <b>{c.label}</b>
          <span class="b">{c.blurb}</span>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ind tsec">
    <h2 id="checked">What the check found</h2>
    <p class="prose">Every homepage in the catalogue was fetched on {checked}, twice: once plainly,
      then again in a real browser for the ones that refused. That is where the descriptions come
      from, and it is the only way to find out that something is gone.</p>
    <p class="prose"><b>{unreachable.length} of {tools.length} did not answer.</b> Some are dead
      domains, some return 404, some present an expired certificate, and a few simply refuse
      automated requests and are probably fine. Each one says which. They stay listed, struck
      through, because nothing here is deleted.</p>
    <ul class="tl">
      {unreachable.map((t) => (
        <li>
          <div class="h"><a href={`/tools/${t.slug}/`} class="gone-link">{t.name}</a>
            <span class="gone">{t.live.reason}</span></div>
          <div class="d">{t.desc}</div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="prose"><b>{moved.length} answered from a different address than the one listed.</b>
      Some are ordinary redirects. Others are acquisitions, renames, or a domain that changed hands
      and now sells something else entirely. Each tool's page names where it actually led.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ind tsec">
    <h2 id="graph">On the word "cloud"</h2>
    <p class="prose">This was asked for as a cloud, with every tool wired to the ones it relates to.
      The data does not support that drawing and the honest thing is to say so here rather than to
      invent the wiring.</p>
    <p class="prose">An edge means one entry names another by name. That yields {edges} edges across
      {' '}{tools.length} tools, and only {withGraph} of the fourteen categories have enough of them
      to draw. Products are described on their own terms, so the text almost never says what a thing
      works with. Where a graph exists it is on the category page. Where it does not, that page says
      so and lists the tools instead. A hundred nodes wired by shared category would look like a
      cloud and would mean nothing.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ind tsec">
    <h2 id="how">How this was built</h2>
    <p class="prose">The list of names was assembled from public directories of developer tools.
      Nothing else came from them: every description, every line about what a tool is sold for, and
      every judgement about where it sits was written here from the product's own homepage.</p>
    <p class="prose dim">Re-running the check is one command, and the results are committed rather
      than fetched at build time. A page that reaches the network to render is a page that depends
      on a stranger's uptime.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="foot"><span>Imran</span><span>Tools</span><span>[Catalogue]</span></div>
</Doc>
